## Problem

CMP 100HX cards suffer severe prompt processing (PP) degradation on large MMQ tile kernels 
(ubatch >= ~64). ubatch 56 bypasses this and recovers ~2x PP throughput, setting ubatch=56 globally in a hybrid setup (CMP + normal GPU) causes ~3.5x PP througput regression on the paired normal GPU.

Previous discussion showed that a ubatch of 56 on the CMP100hx card seem optimal. 
https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/discussions/15013#discussioncomment-17059084

There is no existing runtime flag that resolves this conflict — llama.cpp 
uses a single global ubatch for all devices.

## Hardware Tested

- CMP 100HX-210 with V100 firmware (detected as Tesla V100-PCIE-12GB)
- Paired with Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB
- Stock CMP 100HX detected via "CMP" or "100HX" device name strings
- Tensor split syntax in ik_llama.cpp uses `/` not `,`

## Root Cause Evidence

Running Gemma 4 12B Q4_K_XL on CMP 100HX alone:

| ubatch | pp2048 | tg512 | GPU util | Avg power |
|--------|--------|-------|----------|-----------|
| 2048 (large tiles) | 279 t/s | 34 t/s | 98% | 98W |
| 56 (small tiles) | 598 t/s | 34 t/s | 98% | 116W |

Same GPU utilization, similar power draw, 2x different throughput — 
consistent with dispatch throttling not hardware removal.

RTX 2080 Ti for comparison:

| ubatch | pp2048 | tg512 |
|--------|--------|-------|
| 2048 | 1784 t/s | 58 t/s |
| 56 | 1109 t/s | 58 t/s |

Normal hardware shows opposite behavior — large tiles are faster.
TG is unaffected on both cards (bandwidth bound).

## Fix

Intercept `ggml_cuda_mul_mat` in `ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu` and 
automatically chunk large matmuls to 56 tokens on CMP devices only.

Key design decisions:
- **Cached device detection** — `cudaGetDeviceProperties` called once per 
  thread not per matmul (critical for MoE models with thousands of matmul 
  calls per token)
- **ne0 >= 5120 threshold** — skips MoE expert layers (ne0=512/2048) 
  automatically, only chunks large dense FFN/attention matrices
- **Anti-recursion guard** — `thread_local bool is_chunking` prevents 
  infinite recursion
- **Normal GPUs unaffected** — device name check ensures P100/2080Ti/etc 
  never enter the chunking path

```cpp
static void ggml_cuda_mul_mat(ggml_backend_cuda_context & ctx, 
    const ggml_tensor * src0, const ggml_tensor * src1, ggml_tensor * dst) {
    // ==========================================================================
    // CMP 100HX ANTI-RECURSION FRONT GATE CHUNKER
    // ==========================================================================
    static thread_local bool is_chunking = false;
    static thread_local int cached_device_id = -1;
    static thread_local bool cached_is_cmp = false;
    int current_id;
    cudaGetDevice(&current_id);
    if (current_id != cached_device_id) {
        cudaDeviceProp prop;
        cudaGetDeviceProperties(&prop, current_id);
        cached_is_cmp = (strstr(prop.name, "CMP")  != NULL ||
                         strstr(prop.name, "100HX") != NULL ||
                         strstr(prop.name, "Tesla V100-PCIE-12GB") != NULL);
        cached_device_id = current_id;
    }
    bool is_cmp_card = cached_is_cmp;
    if (is_cmp_card && src1->ne[1] > 56 && !is_chunking && src0->ne[0] >= 5120) {
        is_chunking = true;
        const int64_t total_tokens = src1->ne[1];
        const int64_t chunk_size   = 56;
        size_t src1_stride = src1->nb[1];
        size_t dst_stride  = dst->nb[1];
        ggml_tensor * mutable_src1 = const_cast<ggml_tensor *>(src1);
        const int64_t orig_src1_ne1 = src1->ne[1];
        const int64_t orig_dst_ne1  = dst->ne[1];
        mutable_src1->ne[1] = chunk_size;
        dst->ne[1]          = chunk_size;
        for (int64_t i = 0; i < total_tokens; i += chunk_size) {
            if (i + chunk_size > total_tokens) {
                mutable_src1->ne[1] = total_tokens - i;
                dst->ne[1]          = total_tokens - i;
            }
            char * src1_ptr_backup = (char *) src1->data;
            char * dst_ptr_backup  = (char *) dst->data;
            mutable_src1->data = (char *)src1->data + (i * src1_stride);
            dst->data          = (char *)dst->data  + (i * dst_stride);
            ggml_cuda_mul_mat(ctx, src0, src1, dst);
            mutable_src1->data = src1_ptr_backup;
            dst->data          = dst_ptr_backup;
        }
        mutable_src1->ne[1] = orig_src1_ne1;
        dst->ne[1]          = orig_dst_ne1;
        is_chunking = false;
        return;
    }
    // ==========================================================================
```

## Results

Tested on CMP 100HX + P100 dual GPU, `-b 2048 -ub 2048 -p 2048`:

| Model | Type | Before | After | Delta |
|-------|------|--------|-------|-------|
| Qwen3 27B Q4_K_M | Dense 27B | 171 t/s | 269 t/s | **+57%** |
| Gemma 12B Q4_K_XL | Dense 12B | 300 t/s | 332 t/s | **+11%** |
| Llama 2 7B Q4_0 | Dense 7B | 541 t/s | 601 t/s | **+11%** |
| Gemma E2B Q4_0 | Dense 2B | 1044 t/s | 1088 t/s | **+4%** |
| Qwen3 35B MoE | MoE 35B | 473 t/s | 465 t/s | **neutral** |
| Gemma 26B MoE | MoE 26B | 606 t/s | 609 t/s | **neutral** |

Benefits scale with model size and density. MoE models are unaffected 
(their expert matrices are below the ne0 threshold).

## Key Insight

This fix dissolves the hybrid GPU ubatch dilemma.  The global -ub micro-batch setting no longer penalizes the normal GPU in a hybrid setup—the CMP card silently chunks large dense matrices to 56 underneath. No per-model tuning needed, no compromise between cards.

## Notes

- Significantly lower overhead and less complex than running multiple llama.cpp server instances just to isolate device-specific ubatch sizes.
- The `Tesla V100-PCIE-12GB` name string catches CMP 100HX cards flashed 
  with V100 firmware. Stock CMP 100HX cards are caught by `CMP` or `100HX`.
- ne0 threshold of 5120 was determined empirically by logging matrix 
  dimensions across dense and MoE models. Adjust if your model family has 
  different FFN d

## Overview

This PR adds XOR swizzling for K/V shared-memory tiles in the CUDA flash-attention fp16 MMA kernel (fattn-mma-f16.cuh), replacing row padding (+4) as the primary fix for shared-memory bank conflicts on cp.async stores and ldmatrix loads. On Turing+ (Ampere/Ada/Blackwell), when the K/V tile stride is a power-of-two ≥ 32, the kernel uses a pow2 stride with per-row XOR address remapping instead of padded row stride. 

## Additional information

Followed resource : https://lubits.ch/flash/Part-4

This change helped in improving perf for very high context, collected data for 65K depth

